<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kafa Zone</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kafa Zone is an administrative zone of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, with its capital at Bonga. It covers much of the territory of the former Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> in 1897, and its montane forests are home to wild Arabica <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, from whose name the word 'coffee' is often said to derive. Part of the forest is protected as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve.</p></div>
